Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
>
> Whether those variants are "the future", I don't know;

I also don't know if SporkHack or UnNetHack are the future of NetHack.

But in 2010 they are here, whereas NetHack's notorious $NEXT_VERSION
isn't here since 7 years.

> software that
> heavily depends on just one person (development but mainly design) is
> not the most reliable basis for a long lasting development path that

On the other hand, obviously even a large team can't prevent that the
development stops.

> deserves the prediction "it's the future". (I seem to recall that e.g.
> the level compiler enhancements have been implemented by Pasi(?) and
> probably others contributed, too. Derek and Patric, resp., may answer

Pasi has rewritten the level compiler all by himself. I just find bugs
in it. :)

> it more accurately who's involved in which tasks of the development
> and design process.) Folks get involved in their Real World tasks and
> we will have to expect a similar decline as in Nethack, probably in a
> even shorter time span in case of a smaller development/design team.

That might be true but both UnNetHack and SporkHack have the advantage
of a public repository. Even if Derek, Pasi and I get simultaneously
hit by lightning the code we worked on is still there.

And not only the code of the last release version but also the
development code. That's an advantage of public development.


The DevTeam's way of doing development of an open source game is
antique.

If we never see the next version all the work they have done will be
lost. And all the patches and bug reports that have been sent to them.

I will always prefer development in the open to development in the
closet. Nothing is as frustrating as reporting a bug and hearing
"yeah, we fixed that but I won't give you the source".

